<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joanna I of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1326">1326</a>–<a href="/years/1382">1382</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joanna I was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and countess of Provence from 1343 to 1382. Her reign was troubled by the murder of her first husband and wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. She sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in 1348 and her rights over Prato to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1351. Having adopted Louis I of Anjou as heir, she was deposed and murdered by her cousin Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joanna-i-of-naples.jpg" alt="Joanna I of Naples" width="269" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanna_I_di_Napoli_-_Niccol%C3%B2_di_Tommaso_(1360_circa).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joanna was the eldest surviving daughter of Charles, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, only surviving son of King Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and Marie of Valois, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a>. Her father died in 1328, and three years later Robert named Joanna his heir and ordered his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to swear fealty to her. He arranged her marriage to Andrew, younger son of his nephew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a>, who also hoped to secure the inheritance for Andrew. On his deathbed in 1343, however, Robert named Joanna sole heir and set up a regency council to govern until her twenty-first birthday.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Robert died on 20 January 1343, Andrew was knighted and the marriage consummated, but the couple rarely met except at ceremonies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> described them as two lambs entrusted to a multitude of wolves. Andrew's mother, Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in the summer of 1343 to press for his coronation, which Joanna delayed. Joanna's sister Maria was betrothed to Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> in March 1343, and he then abducted her and had her secretly married, to the outrage of the dowager queen Catherine of Valois.</p><p>Andrew was murdered in 1345, and the Hungarians openly accused Joanna when she announced she would marry Louis of Taranto. She married him on 22 August 1347 without the papal dispensation their close kinship required. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> invaded to avenge his brother, and a further Hungarian offensive reached the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1350. Louis of Taranto took control of the government and was recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> as co-ruler on 23 March 1352. After his death Joanna married James IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1363 and later Otto of Brunswick-Grubenhagen on 25 March 1376, but none of her children survived her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the Western Schism Joanna supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> papacy, and Pope Urban VI declared her a heretic and usurper on 11 May 1380. She had arranged the marriage of her niece Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> to Charles of Durazzo to settle the succession, but Charles captured and imprisoned her. She was moved to the castle of Muro Lucano and killed on 27 July 1382. Charles claimed she died of natural causes; two early sources say she was strangled or smothered, and no eyewitness account survives. Excommunicated, she could not be buried in consecrated ground, and her body was thrown into a well at Santa Chiara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><p>The kingdom was left to decades of wars of succession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> wrote her biography in his De mulieribus claris, defending her right to rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a> the elder made her the subject of a romance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joanna I of Naples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1377 Queen <b>Joanna I of Naples</b> leased the principality to the Knights Hospitaller, who brought in the Navarrese Company, and its captains made themselves masters of much of the country, with Pedro de San Superano as prince from 1396.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1351 <b>Queen Joanna I of Naples</b> sold her rights over Prato to the Republic of Florence, which ruled it from then on.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-prato">Commune of Prato</a></li><li><blockquote>During the war of succession that followed Queen <b>Joanna I</b>'s death, Nice and its hinterland rejected the Angevin claimant and in 1388, in the act known as the Dedition, placed themselves under Amadeus VII of Savoy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nice">County of Nice</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Queen Joanna I</b> adopted Louis I of Anjou, whose line held Provence until 1481.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-provence">County of Provence</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Muro Lucano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1362" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joanna I of Naples"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5912 4022h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5912 4022h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1362">What was here in 1362? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
